<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinando I de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1549–<a href="/years/1609">1609</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinando I de' Medici was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> from 1587 to 1609. A cardinal who gave up his office to succeed his brother Francesco I, he governed ably, developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> as a free port open to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and other foreign merchants through the Livornine laws of 1591–1593, drained marshes and reduced Tuscany's dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-of-lorraine">Christina of Lorraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici.jpg" alt="Ferdinando I de' Medici" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scipione Pulzone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pulzone,_Scipione_-_Ferdinando_I_de%27_Medici,_granduca_di_Toscana_-_1590.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
